Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
hello the site of some russian troops  appearing to pull away from the border  with ukraine has been met with some  skepticism as diplomatic efforts to  avoid war continue the russian president  and german chancellor opened the latest  line of dialogue with both saying  conflict can be avoided but as torso  jabari reports from moscow more  conversations are needed before the  chance of war retreats  russia's defense ministry says these are  some of its troops which had been  positioned on the border with ukraine  they are returning to base after  finishing training exercises  as the training comes to an end the  troops as usual will march back to their  home stations units from the southern  and western military districts that  completed their tasks already have  started loading on trains and motor  vehicles they will start moving to their  home bases individual units will march  on their own in military columns  while this raises hopes that tensions  could ease between russia and ukraine  there are still large-scale military  exercises taking place  and it's not clear how many units are  being pulled back  news of the troops drawdown came just as  the german chancellor olof schultz  arrived in russia for talks with  president vladimir putin who said war  was the last thing he wanted  do we want war or not of course we don't  and it's for that reason that we have  submitted a proposal as to a negotiation  process as a result of which there  should be a guarantee for security for  everybody including our country  chancellor olaf schultz said some  aspects of russia's demands on ukraine  were worth considering while stressing  the need for diplomacy to succeed the  diplomatism  today i fully agree that diplomatic  efforts have yet to be exhausted  we have to find a peaceful resolution to  this crisis  several troops have been pulled back  which is a good sign and we hope that  this will continue in the belgian  capital nato secretary general jen  stoltenberg expressed cautious optimism  at the news of the russian drawdown  there are signs from moscow that  diplomacy should continue  this gives  grounds for cautious optimism  but so far  we have not seen any sign of  de-escalation on the ground despite  these intense diplomatic efforts there  are still many outstanding issues at the  heart of the crisis  vladimir putin says he is ready to talk  to u.s leaders about ukraine's request  to join the 30-member nato alliance and  he says that is a conversation which  cannot be put off any longer something  both nato and u.s leaders have refused  to discuss so far  and until that issue is resolved it is  now clear that this crisis could  continue for months to come  dorsal jabari al jazeera moscow  ukraine and western leaders say they  haven't seen enough evidence that  russian troops are withdrawing the  circles are where russia is thought to  have deployed more than 130 000 troops  surrounding ukraine on three sides  russian soldiers also deployed in the  annexed region of crimea and back  thousands of fighters in two rebel-held  eastern provinces  hours before russia's withdrawal  announcement a u.s defense official said  ground units were moving closer to the  ukrainian border a satellite company  said there was increased military  activity in belarus crimea and western  russia including the arrival of  helicopters and fighter jets  the uk says russia is building field  hospitals near ukraine in belarus which  could mean an invasion is being planned  it also says battalion tactical groups  are being taken closer to the border  step vassen is in luninette in southern  belarus one of the places russia has  been building up troops she gave us this  update earlier  i have to say there's quite some  skepticism here also in the south of  belarus about this statement that the  russian troops are leaving or about to  leave it's also very hard to verify this  statement here on the ground because  there has been some footage released by  the russian ministry of defense showing  tanks and troops leaving but there  wasn't any mention of date or time or  location we're here at the london yet  air base which is one of the locations  where reportedly high-end equipment from  russia was brought in as far as 10 000  kilometers away from the far east the  eastern command apparently s 400 missile  defense systems are here and also sukhai  sukhoi fighter jets well we know now  that this exercise or whatever is  happening here hasn't ended because just  uh 20 minutes ago we heard a fighter  jets taken to the air presumably on a  training mission  so definitely activity here still in  lunan yet and it's also interesting to  note that the russian ministry said that  the western and southern command was  going to be withdrawn back to their  bases in russia but they didn't mention  anything about the eastern and central  command and what we've known so far is  that there are many troops brought in  from the far east  the ukrainian government says the  websites of its defence foreign and  culture ministries as well as the two  largest state banks have been taken  offline by a cyber attack  inside ukraine anxiety is building as  residents consider what to do if russia  launches a ground invasion natasha  butler reports from kiev  slava says his son ivan brings him joy  in what he says is a world of hysteria  he doesn't want to leave his home in  kiev but says he'd consider doing so to  protect his family if russia were to  invade ukraine  every day i'm thinking about what to do  with my family or as i go to view for  example to western part of ukraine or go  to village where my  parents of my wife  live they have small house but at least  they have  for example they have electric generator  they have solar panels so at least we'll  have electricity for example the fridge  will be working  most people in the city are tired of  thinking about what russia might do next  for eight years they've lived with  conflict in the east of the country  between ukrainian forces and  russian-backed separatists but the  recent crisis is fueling a renewed sense  of anxiety  things seem very normal in the city  people are still going to work or to  school the shops and restaurants are  busy but as the situation continues some  people that we meet tell us that they  are worried and making contingency plans  if there is an invasion we will go to  western ukraine but if there is a need  to fight my entire family will  russians are just showing off making  noise play acting russians aren't only  influencing ukrainian opinion but  everyone else but russia will destroy  itself if it invades  diplomatic efforts to avert a new war  have seemed at times to go round in  circles with little sign of a  breakthrough moscow now says it's  pulling some troops from the border but  ukraine's government is skeptical  we continuously hear different  statements from the russian federation  so we have a rule we believe not what we  hear but we believe what we see  if we see the pull out we will believe  in the escalation  40 years ago this huge arch was built on  a hill in the city to celebrate russia  and ukraine's friendship since then  someone has painted a large crack in the  monument  no one here wants a new conflict but  history has taught them to be prepared  natasha butler al jazeera  in the next couple of hours u.s  president joe biden is due to speak  about ukraine let's get more from our  white house correspondent kimberly  hellkit and kimberly what are we  expecting to hear from him  well according to the white house press  secretary jensocki the u.s president is  expected to address the american public  in the world from the east room of the  white house at about 20 30 gmt he is  expected to talk about the situation on  the ground in ukraine as well as the  steps that have been taken thus far to  avert war and the actions that the west  is prepared to take should russia invade  ukraine now  in addition to this we expect that the  u.s president will talk about what is at  stake for the united states and the  world if this conflict is not averted  and how americans could potentially face  repercussions here in the united states  something that many americans really  have not thought much about of and this  is certainly going to be a bit of a  wake-up call for some who are watching  or thinking about this for the first  time now in the interim the president  prior to  making the speech has been speaking to  world leaders at least he did speak to  the french president emmanuel macron we  don't have a readout of that call yet  but we do know that his secretary of  state anthony blinken was speaking with  his counterpart his russian counterpart  sergey lavrov the foreign minister and  we now have a readout of that telephone  call  we understand that the united states  once again reiterating that they are  pushing for a diplomatic solution to  resolve these security differences they  emphasize the need for a verifiable  credible and meaningful de-escalation  of what they call russian aggression  that buildup of russian troops along  ukraine's borders further the united  states said that should russia choose  the path of invading ukraine there would  be swift severe and a united  transatlantic response the united states  at this hour believing that there is  still a window to resolve this crisis  peacefully but we expect when we hear  from the president he will say that that  window is soon going to be closing  kimberly hackett thank you very much  indeed

